In this lesson we'll look at how sine and cosine can describe anything that waves or oscillates, like tides, sound, or daily temperature. The trick is that one neat rule packs in all the information you need. First, the amplitude tells you how big the swing is — how far the tide rises and falls, for example. Next, the period tells you how long one full cycle takes, so a twelve-hour tide cycle means the pattern repeats every twelve hours. Then there's the mean value, which is just the centre line the motion wobbles around, like mean sea level or the average temperature for the day. Finally, the phase shift tells you when the cycle starts — whether the first peak happens right away or after a delay. So when you see a formula like the depth of water in a harbour written as one-point-five sine of pi over six times t minus two, plus five, you can pull it apart easily. The amplitude is one-point-five metres, the period is twelve hours, the mean depth is five metres, and high tide arrives at the five-hour mark. Now let's see it in action.